Package: urbanwelfare
Type: Package
Title: Sufficient-Statistics Welfare Incidence and Optimal Policy in Urban Networks
Version: 0.1.0
Authors@R: c(
    person("Treb", "Allen", role = "aut", email = "treb.allen@dartmouth.edu"),
    person("Simon", "Fuchs", role = c("aut", "cre"), email = "simon.fuchs@atl.frb.org"),
    person("Sharat", "Ganapati", role = "aut", email = "sg1390@georgetown.edu"),
    person("Alberto", "Graziano", role = "aut"),
    person("Rocio", "Madera", role = "aut", email = "romadera@smu.edu"),
    person("Judit", "Montoriol-Garriga", role = "aut")
  )
Description: Implements the sufficient-statistics methodology of Allen, Fuchs,
    Ganapati, Graziano, Madera, and Montoriol-Garriga (2026, "Urban Welfare:
    Tourism in Barcelona") for measuring the spatial welfare incidence of
    localized demand shocks within cities from bilateral spending, income, and
    commuting records without unit price indices or parametric functional forms.
    Provides validated open-city spatial accounting ('urban_account'), network
    exposure construction and Borusyak-Hull shift-share controls
    ('build_exposures'), high-dimensional bilateral panel estimation wrapping
    'fixest' ('estimate_bilateral'), producer price-share matching and open-city
    common-level anchoring ('recover_welfare'), robustness extensions for CES
    shopping residuals, Frechet workplace mobility, and finite-change linear
    programming bounds, plus an optional C++-accelerated nonlinear quantitative
    spatial model (QSM) and adjoint Ramsey spatial tax solver
    ('optimal_spatial_tax') and synthetic city simulator ('sim_urban_city').
